= Test set =

== Overview ==
A '''test set''' is held-out data used to estimate a system's performance after training and development choices have been fixed.

Its purpose is independent evaluation.

== Independence ==
A test set provides a cleaner performance estimate when its examples, labels, and aggregate results do not influence model fitting or design.

Direct or indirect influence can turn testing into development.

== Repeated use ==
Repeatedly checking a public or private test score and changing the system in response can overfit the test set.

The dataset may still be called “test”, but functionally it is participating in model selection.

== Benchmarking ==
Shared benchmarks often serve as public test sets.

Long-term repeated community optimization can cause benchmark saturation, contamination, or selection effects even when individual model training follows nominal splits.

== Final test is not deployment ==
A held-out test set estimates performance only under its sampling and task assumptions.

Real deployment may involve [[Distribution shift]], new subgroups, changed incentives, or different data quality.

== Related concepts ==
[[Training set]], [[Validation set]], [[Generalization (machine learning)]], [[Benchmark contamination]], [[Data leakage (machine learning)]], and [[Evidence]] define the evaluation boundary.

